---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="History of Røvær" description="From the Stone Age and Viking era to fishing community. The history of Røvær." lang="en" page="history">
  <div class="page">
    <h1 class="page-title">History of Røvær</h1>
    <p class="page-intro">
      Røvær has been inhabited for thousands of years. The island bears traces
      of Stone Age people, Vikings and generations of fishermen.
    </p>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Exciting archaeological finds</h2>
      <p>
        There is something about the sheltered coves and the proximity to good
        fishing grounds that has led people, as far back as the Stone Age,
        to settle here.
      </p>
      <p>
        Archaeological excavations in 1988 uncovered a Stone Age settlement,
        burial cairns, stone arrangements and sacrificial finds. At Grønavikjo,
        the remains of a prehistoric fishing village with 14 house foundations
        were found, the oldest dating from the Iron Age to the early Middle Ages.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">A thriving fishing community</h2>
      <p>
        In the mid-1800s, population growth in Norway coincided with rich herring
        years. Røvær attracted settlers due to the short distance to fishing
        grounds and an excellent harbour.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Tragic accident in 1899</h2>
      <img class="section-img" src="/images/sjosproyt_2-500x330.jpg" alt="Sea spray at Røvær">
      <p>
        On 13 October 1899, a disaster struck the island community hard. Thirty
        of the island's approximately 130 inhabitants perished on their way home
        from a funeral in Haugesund. The accident left 12 widows with 36
        fatherless, unconfirmed children, and triggered a national fundraising
        campaign.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Population trends</h2>
      <p>
        Røvær had its highest population around 1950, with approximately 170
        permanent residents. As the herring fishery declined, the population
        gradually decreased. Today, Røvær is a small but vibrant island community.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
